<%= colour_example('chart-govuk-multiple', layout: :chart, callout: false) %>

### Divergent categories

Hours each room spent below or above its heating target in one week. Notice that blues mean below the target and reds mean above it, with darker steps further from the target.

<%= colour_example('chart-govuk-divergent', layout: :chart, callout: false) %>

## Using charts within social media

In a social media post, such as one sharing a household's year of solar power, use larger, bolder shapes so people take in the message quickly. You can use an additional colour to pick out one result, on a background that uses a 25% or 50% shade. Do not use this style on a dashboard.

The example shows the most-used automations in a month, as a post might share them.

<%= colour_example('chart-govuk-social', layout: :chart) %>

<h2 id="give-each-series-a-stable-colour">Give each device a stable colour</h2>

A device's colour must not change when you add another device, hide a series or change the theme colour. For example, the dishwasher keeps its colour whether or not the oven is shown.

Pass the device's stable entity ID or statistic ID to `chartSeriesStyle(key, {mode})` from `design-system/chart-colours.mjs`. It returns one of the 6 categorical colours with a fill pattern, line dash and marker. The same ID always gives the same style, after a reload, a reorder or a legend change, so you do not need to save a colour for each device. Keep the ID when the friendly name changes. A previous-period comparison, and the positive and negative halves of a power source, share their base ID.

Energy roles have fixed colours:

| Energy role | Colour | Pattern |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Grid import | Primary blue | Solid |
| Grid export | Blue shade 50% | Solid |
| Solar | Orange tint 25% | Solid |
| Gas | Primary magenta | Solid |
| Water | Teal tint 25% | Solid |
| Non-fossil energy | Teal tint 25% | Diagonal |
| Battery charging | Purple tint 25% | Solid |
| Battery discharge | Purple tint 25% | Reverse diagonal |
| Total consumption | Black (White in dark mode) | Solid, with a dashed line |

Several meters in one role, such as 2 gas meters, use keys like `gas:<statistic ID>`. They keep the role colour and each gets its own pattern.

For charts that accept only numbered colours, the theme supplies `civica-chart-1` to `civica-chart-24`. The first 9 follow the energy roles in the table, and the rest repeat the 6 categorical colours. 24 is not a limit on the number of series.

The 6 colours cannot tell more than 6 devices apart, so colours repeat and the pattern, line dash and marker do the rest. The adapter adds them to the native legend and keeps its controls to show and hide each series. When a chart gets crowded, show fewer series and keep every value in the **View data** table. Never drop readings or merge devices to stay under a limit. The [24-device chart](../components/chart-examples.html) shows this.

This example shows the keys for 3 appliances in light and dark mode. Notice that 2 of them share a colour, and that each keeps the same style in both modes.

<%= example('data-colour-categorical') %>

<h2 id="bg-col-07">Keep chart and map scales independent</h2>

The theme colour must never change how a grid, solar or device series looks.

<h3 id="choose-the-colour-mapping">Choose colours from what the data means</h3>

| What the colour shows | Colours | What the chart also needs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A source, such as a device | The [categorical palette](#categorical-palette) | A stable ID, a readable name and a pattern, line style or marker |
| An amount that goes up in one direction | The tints and shades of one [scale family](#sequential-and-divergent-scale-palette) | The measure, unit, lowest and highest values, and what happens outside them |
| A difference above or below a centre | Two scale families with a neutral centre | A centre that means something, such as a target temperature, and signed values |
| No reading | None | Words such as “Not supplied” and a mark that is not a number |

A categorical colour is not a rank or an amount: do not use the categorical colours to show temperatures rising. A real zero belongs on the scale. An unknown or unavailable reading does not.

### Define a scale before you choose colours

For a heatmap or a map layer that shows an amount, write down the quantity, unit, period, lowest and highest values and any centre first. Then use the listed steps of one family, from light to dark. If a value falls outside the scale, say so: giving it the end colour hides it. If the range changes, update the key and say whether charts from different periods can still be compared.

Keep labels outside coloured areas unless you have tested the text on that fill. Pale steps can disappear on a white background, so add outlines and direct labels. Give a missing value a crossed empty swatch and the words “Not supplied”, never a colour from the scale.

This example shows energy used in each room on a 0 to 2 kWh scale, and room temperatures as a difference from a 20 °C target. Notice that the missing reading has no colour from the scale.

<%= example('data-colour-quantitative') %>

## In Home Assistant

The optional civica companion applies these colours to the native Energy dashboard charts. [Charts and tables](../components/charts.html) owns the rest of the chart specification, such as line widths and the dark-mode labels, and [Chart examples](../components/chart-examples.html) has working charts. [Data visualisation](brand-data.html) explains how to choose a chart.

To change a chart colour or energy role, see [Maintain the tokens](colour.html#maintain-the-tokens).
